  "notes": "\"Emo cowgirl\" — 90s-female-country phrasing and steel over modern clarity, and the entry credits her with \"the most consistent tempo/key fingerprint of the 2020s cohort.\" Both halves are authored as stated: \"B♭ major is overwhelmingly her home key, with A/A♭/F/B and minor cuts in G minor/D♭ minor,\" and the ballad-heavy core is notated \"~71–78 with the backbeat on 2&4\" with the recurring 142/150 readings being the doubled values, so the mode sits at 75 in a 71–135 window. Drums are \"cross-stick or brush verses with shaker, snare entering at the chorus, kick on 1 and 3, a programmed clap under the snare on the radio mixes, low fill density,\" which is a quiet kit with a clap layer and a long fill grid. Harmony carries a period habit the entry names: \"90s-style V7 approach chords,\" which is the raised seventh share against otherwise plain I–V–vi–IV and I–IV–I–V. The counter is the entry's own instrument call — \"pedal steel is the primary answering voice\" — so the answer lick outweighs everything else. Melody is \"mid-alto, conversational and diary-like in the verse with a narrow range, chorus a fifth or sixth up on a repeated cell; a deliberate country break at phrase ends, no melisma.\" ⚠ The sub is described as \"light reinforcement only,\" so no `drums.bass808` block is authored and the model keeps the live bass with its walking pickups. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: medium-high); the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the clap offsets, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
